in the very south of poland near the  slovak border you'll find impressive  mountain landscapes  this is the world of mountain guide  pyotr konopka  he has climbed peaks all over the world  in the andes the himalayas  the rockies  but his favorite mountain is right here  in the high tatras it's called  rizzi  the tatara are probably the lowest range  of mountains in the world  the landscape has the feel of serious  tall mountains but in fact the highest  peak is just 2655 meters  konopka has been guiding visitors here  professionally for more than 30 years  in his free time he loves to go climbing  it's all very accessible there are  well-equipped huts in every valley  and the views are good  andrei wapsa also enjoys the views as he  pilots tourists around the high tetras  it is harder flying in the mountains  because the weather keeps changing all  the time  today for example it is really windy and  there's lots of turbulence  but all the challenges are offset by the  amazing views  zakopane is a famous resort for winter  sports  nearby is coho the village is famous for  its many traditional wooden houses from  the 19th century  the village has had landmark status for  more than 50 years  croco wolf is on the charney dunayas  river which feeds into the donnayats  river  a great way to explore the region is by  raft  the tradition of rafting grew out of  lagging timber was sent down the dunayas  into the vistula  and then all the way to the baltic later  goods were transported by raft and  nowadays people are  where the dunyach flows into georgeton  lake stands nizitza castle  it was built in the 14th century by a  hungarian nobleman  for centuries it served as a border post  between poland and hungary  one of the attractions is a well 60  meters deep  there is a legend about this well brun  hilde and bogaswav once lived in this  castle  they fought a lot and one day in anger  bogaswav threw brunhilda down the well  she came back to haunt him as a ghost  but she eventually  now both of them are said to haunt the  castle  we now head north away from the  mountains to bochnia a town famous for  its salt mine  the mine began operations in the 13th  century mining ended in the late 20th  century  some of the galleries and chambers deep  below the surface are now open to  visitors  last year unesco declared the mine a  world heritage site  mukash pannek is a tour guide he knows  all the old legends about the mine  this is our salt garden it often drips  here  and if a drop of water lands on your  head it will make you cleverer  if it lands on your outstretched hand  you'll become rich  but if it lands on your neck or back  you'll have to toil  and you'll become a hunchback  meanwhile andrei wapsa is on his last  tour of the day  i think about flying every day and i  always want to be up in the sky  it is so wonderful  especially when you're flying above the  beautiful landscapes of southern poland  you
 

